{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293286 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293287 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293288 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293289 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293290 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1041 "selection" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293287 13194139534529 true] [17592186293285 1110 #uuid "fb9f02f3-5a5b-5422-b093-4ec199edaa9d" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293286 1034 "x\\ge 1" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534529 true] [17592186293286 1111 #uuid "187d3e63-b629-50df-8c9f-8c5c6b4e14fc" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293287 1034 "0<x\\le 2" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534529 true] [17592186293287 1111 #uuid "02689765-5087-5434-b9cd-898e90fe2759" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293288 1034 "x>0" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534529 true] [17592186293288 1111 #uuid "b2d21f04-20c5-5e88-898a-07eecd9e3fcb" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293289 1034 "0<x\\le 1" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534529 true] [17592186293289 1111 #uuid "85aadba4-72e1-562a-a0a9-f3856aaf1d09" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293290 1034 "x\\ge 2" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534529 true] [17592186293290 1111 #uuid "28b067f0-d15a-543e-adeb-c4d5e34f88b0" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293292 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1089 "q-141310" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1100 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1113 #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293291 1145 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293293 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293294 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293295 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293296 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293297 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1041 "selection" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293293 13194139534529 true] [17592186293292 1110 #uuid "643857a5-9540-59d0-a43f-59fcc5c1d814" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293293 1034 "Converges to $0$" 13194139534529 true] [17592186293293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534529 true] [175921